WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:09.810
MARKUS KLUTE:
Welcome back to 871.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:13.680
So in this section of our
discussion of instrumentation,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.079
we talk about
accelerators, and I'll

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.590
do this in a little bit of
an historic way showing you

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:25.750
some of the developments over
the last up to 100 years.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.960
We use electromagnetic
fields in order

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.010
to accelerate charged particles,
and so the developments

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.030
in electromagnetic and
understanding electromagnetism

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.820
led to then the
technical developments

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.820
or the technological
development of accelerators

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.970
and the availability
of devices, which

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:52.460
can be used in order to
accelerate or modify particles.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.090
And so this goes back
to Maxwell and Hertz

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.580
discovering
electromagnetic waves

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.990
towards JJ Thomson,
who was able to use

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:06.580
cathode rays and the
classical Lorentz force

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.910
in order to understand the
electromagnetic fields.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.980
If we study particle
accelerators,

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.830
we can see three
different historic lines--

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.820
direct voltage accelerators,
resonant accelerators,

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.920
and transformer
accelerators, and we

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.670
go through those three
different subjects one by one.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.570
The energy limits
of our accelerators,

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.450
they are typically given by
the maximum possible voltage

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.910
available.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:42.750
When you think about
accelerators used for colliding

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:48.100
beams, it's not just the energy
which is the limiting factor,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.630
but you also need a
sufficient number of particles

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.420
to be accelerated and
brought to collisions.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.840
And those particles need
to be in some sort of beam,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.860
which is narrow such that
collisions are possible.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.840
Requirements change
depending on whether or not

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.260
you have colliding beams or
fixed target experiments,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.169
whether or not
you actually study

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:13.200
lepton collisions,
or hadron collisions,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.600
whether or not you're
using secondary particles

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.090
as the means of what you're
going to learn from this.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.870
But the concepts are
very comparable across

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.870
individual fields of study.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.660
So let's start with
the van der Meer--

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.910
Van de Graaff accelerator.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.950
So you basically have to be
able to create large voltages,

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.120
and for the Van de
Graaff accelerator,

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.810
it's been doing this,
creating this large voltage,

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:45.290
by just moving particles from
on one level-- separating

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.260
particles to create
this large voltage.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.820
You can typically get up to
megavolts and tens of megavolts

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.960
of voltages, but you
need to make sure

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.420
that you don't enter a
breakdown kind of regime.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.080
And so it depends on what
kind of insulating materials

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:03.980
you use.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:08.840
If you are using insulating
gas under certain environmental

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:14.120
conditions, you get up to
17.5 megavolts, which then can

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.500
be used in order to accelerate.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.610
We can use the fact that you can
use the electric fields twice

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:26.170
in the tandem
accelerators by changing

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.110
the direction of the voltage.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:33.820
Large potential differences
have been field of studies

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:39.310
in the 1920s and
'30s, and one not so--

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:43.300
and it's just noteworthy that
this is a dangerous field.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.760
So Brasch and Lange,
they use potential

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.800
from lightning in
the Swiss Alps,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:53.168
but this was fatal for Lange,
who was electrocuted fatally

00:03:53.168 --> 00:03:54.085
by a lightning strike.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.800
So then you need to
think about how can you

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.230
make large voltages
available, and how can you

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.530
make them available
for acceleration?

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:08.330
Cockcroft-Walton
accelerators use a sequence

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:13.680
of a cascade generator in order
to reuse the voltage in order

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.450
to create a larger
potential for acceleration.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.060
The max generator is very--

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.660
conceptually is
very, very similar.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.080
Those are still
used today, so we

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:32.480
know why those developments
are 80 or 90 years old.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.030
If you go to a Physics
Today article from 2003,

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.650
you see that a
machine like this is

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:42.500
being used in order to create an
initial confinement for fusion

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.385
reactors, and the total
power is rather fascinating.

00:04:46.385 --> 00:04:50.600
It's four terabytes, which can
be released in 100 nanoseconds

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.840
from a larger number of
those Marx generators.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:59.490
In modern particle
physics accelerators,

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:02.670
we use resonant
acceleration quite a lot,

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.920
and the idea is really like
it's shown in this picture

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.100
here, that the
charged particles,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.350
they're kind of being
accelerated on a wave.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.380
So basically, they're surfing
on electromagnetic waves.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.640
And this can be seen here.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.390
You just place them
correctly in your waveform

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.110
on a specific
point, and then they

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.320
can be accelerated
over some distance.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.410
So the key point
here is that you

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:31.750
have a proper or correct phase
relation to the accelerating

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:34.570
voltage, and the set
up can be varying.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.490
And historically,
there's various attempts

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.760
to do this in an optimal way.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.610
The first one is
the cyclotron, which

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:49.260
has a static magnetic
field, and in each turn,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.450
your particles are
being injected,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.000
and then in each
turn, your particle's

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.800
getting a kick here
and a kick here.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.220
And because their
velocity is increasing,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.010
the radius in this
fixed magnetic field

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.580
is increasing as well.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.210
So after some time, the
particle is accelerated

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:10.350
and leaves the cyclotron
as a specific velocity.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:16.885
Focusing here is important.

00:06:16.885 --> 00:06:18.510
You don't want to
just inject particles

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:23.700
and then they spray all over the
place, an accelerator's focus

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.880
and having the right
optics for the particle is

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.110
a key part of the work needed.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.240
There's a number of techniques.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.500
I don't want to go
into too much detail,

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.730
but what you typically
see is if you compress

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.220
the beam in one
direction, it decomposes

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.610
in the other direction.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:46.080
And so quadruple magnets
can be used in order

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:52.405
to get them focusing, and
use fringe field and edge

00:06:52.405 --> 00:06:54.480
field in order to make
sure that the beam itself

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.640
stays in a compact form.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.218
You can also do techniques
where you have initial focusing

00:07:01.218 --> 00:07:03.260
in one direction and then
in the other direction,

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:09.320
so the result can
be made smaller.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.990
Accelerators are not
just important today

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:16.520
in particle physics, but
they have specifically

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.870
found their place in
medical applications,

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.410
in tumor therapy specifically.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.600
And the history
here is very long.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.570
You see those initial
ideas or methods

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:28.220
where neutrons are used--

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:33.110
[INAUDIBLE] neutrons are used
in order to treat tumors.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.420
We haven't discussed this
in nuclear physics too much,

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:39.110
but when you use ions
as a radiation form,

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:42.770
you can really pinpoint
where energy is deposited.

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:44.630
The so-called Bragg
peak is used in order

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:48.590
to precisely figure in
a three-dimensional way

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:52.850
where the energy of the
ions is being deposited.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:56.290
This is in contrast to radiation
therapy with photons, which

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:01.670
just basically spray a larger
part of the light material

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:05.750
and just destroy
the cancerous cells

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:10.690
but also the ones which
are still healthy.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.030
Any given large
hospital nowadays

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:16.450
has a small accelerator, and so
there's thousands and thousands

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:18.280
of those available.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:22.660
And the work and the maintenance
of those is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:27.760
But continuing the discussion
of accelerator concepts,

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.610
these kind of
racetrack accelerators

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:32.049
are quite interesting.

00:08:32.049 --> 00:08:34.429
You basically have
a couple of fixed--

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:37.419
you have your particles
being injected,

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:40.360
and it's getting,
like in the cyclotron,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.179
larger and larger kicks,
and then, at some point,

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:46.490
can be injected in
order to do experiments.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.360
Again, this is
not the technology

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.990
which is not new anymore.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:55.385
MAMI is an accelerator at the
University of Mainz, and it's--

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.890
the next generation is
using-- the next generation

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:04.580
of accelerators at this facility
is using a similar technology.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:13.070
The question is what
kind of conditions

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.030
you have to fulfill in order
to keep the particles in place,

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:22.270
and here because the particle
moves along, it, in each turn,

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:25.150
takes more time in order
to make one circulation.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:28.390
So what you want to make
sure is that your phase

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.740
and the acceleration
stays in sync,

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.470
so you want to make sure that
the particular event comes

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.860
around again, gets another kick,
and it's not deaccelerated.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.250
And so that explains
why in those machines

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.510
the particles are bunched.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:45.730
They come in little
blocks of particles

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.550
instead of just being a
continuous stream of particles,

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.910
and you can work out the
conditions necessary in order

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:54.850
to fulfill the requirement
that particles are

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:58.740
being continuously accelerated.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.160
So then there's-- over
the history a number

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:07.200
of technologies which try to
make use of the fact that when

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.470
you accelerate, the velocity
of the particles increases,

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:16.710
and so initially, at a specific
time, with one sequence,

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.420
the particle gets a kick.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.440
And then in the next
time, it's already faster.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.930
It travels further, so you can--

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.700
[INAUDIBLE] a linear
accelerator structure,

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.780
so you just increase the length
of each accelerator structure

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.010
in order to make sure that
you, again, can give particles

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.090
the necessary kick.

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:42.590
Nowadays, they use cavities, and
we use superconducting cavities

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:44.630
in order to make them
energy efficient in order

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.300
to have large gradients.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.210
The general idea is, again,
that you place your particle

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:53.420
in here, and you place
it such in your face

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:57.470
in your electromagnetic
field that it always

00:10:57.470 --> 00:10:59.990
gets a kick instead of
being on the other side

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:03.400
and being deaccelerated.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:07.250
Again, the Alvarez Linac
is a very similar concept,

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.010
so the advantage
here is that you only

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:10.960
have one power
input, which then you

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.160
couple and the walls of the
machine don't dissipate energy.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:18.830
So then next, once you have an
accelerator structure in place,

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.310
you also want to make sure
that the structure itself

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:24.370
doesn't interfere with a beam,
that the power transfer is

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:28.660
as efficient as possible,
such that you can get more

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.480
for your buck.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:34.270
All right, I mentioned
it a few times already,

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:40.100
the fact when you have
an electromagnetic wave

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.302
of this form and you
put your particle here,

00:11:42.302 --> 00:11:44.510
the question is what happens
to these particles which

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:47.390
have a slightly higher energy
over these particle which

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.320
have a slightly lower energy?

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:51.230
And it turns out that
this kind of wave

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:56.070
has a self-focusing kind
of structure in a sense

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.490
that the particles were
a little bit behind, they

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.400
get a little bit larger kick.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.150
Particles which are a
little bit in front,

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:04.950
they get a little
bit lower kick, which

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.890
means overall in energy
you focus your bunch.

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:13.330
In energy and space,
you focus the bunch.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.430
You can go one step further
using RF quadrupoles.

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:18.470
Again, no details given here.

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:21.650
You use this unit in order
to further squeeze the beam

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:25.580
and reduce the footprint in
energy and space, the phase

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:28.610
space your particles
are occupying.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:38.510
All right, the next level here
is then to use a betatron,

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:44.400
and the idea of the
betatron is that you change

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.020
the magnetic field as you go.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.828
Instead of changing the
size of the structure,

00:12:48.828 --> 00:12:50.370
you change the
magnetic field, so you

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:54.930
can use the same structure in
order to confine your beams.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.540
And the next level to this
is that you don't just

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:58.410
use one magnet.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:01.660
You use many magnets, and
this is done in synchrotrons,

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.940
modern synchrotrons.

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:08.910
So here, again, the large
line in history, but the point

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:13.530
is that when you use the
same orbit for the particles,

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:17.040
the way you accelerate
them, your magnet structures

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.750
can become much, much smaller.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:24.040
So you have many small magnets
instead of one large magnet,

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:27.680
and only one or few accelerator
sections are needed.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.550
So the particle passes by
here as being accelerated,

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:35.210
and then you have magnets,
of course, a link, the ring,

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:40.750
which are able to change
their field strength.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:42.812
Again, if you have
constant field strength,

00:13:42.812 --> 00:13:45.020
the radius changes, but if
you're modifying the field

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:47.530
strength accordingly to
the speed of the particle,

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:52.255
you can keep particles in
the same circular structure.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:57.910
More words on the
focusing, again,

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:03.400
if you space a magnet such that
there's gradient in the fields

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:04.690
between--

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.060
depending on the position,
you can use that fact

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:10.990
in order to have particles
which are further out being

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:12.040
bent more inwards.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:14.660
Particles which are further
inwards, bend more outwards.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.750
This is called
refocusing, and you

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.180
can do this not just
once, but twice,

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.410
by changing the rotation.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:24.860
And that's called
strong focusing.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.450
Synchrotrons have limitations.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.250
There's two which
are rather important.

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:32.900
The first one, the
radius of the synchrotron

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:35.510
is determined by the
momentum of the particle

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:37.380
and your magnetic field.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:40.370
So at some point, you run
into technical limitations

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:42.770
on the magnetic field strength.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.680
Modern superconducting
magnets, they

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:49.580
get up to in the order
of eight, nine tesla.

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:53.540
We are able to produce
accelerator-grade magnets up

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:58.100
to 14 tesla using
superconducting materials.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:01.340
And so that then was a fixed
plot size of your tunnel

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:05.450
of your ring limits the amount
of momentum you can give

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:07.470
or energy you can
give to your particle.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:14.450
And so for the LHC, we
accelerate protons up to 75 TV

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:18.860
in one beam, and we use the
magnetic field of 8.4 tesla.

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.443
So that's really the maximum the
machine can actually deliver,

00:15:21.443 --> 00:15:23.735
and we haven't actually
demonstrated that we can get up

00:15:23.735 --> 00:15:24.350
to this point.

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:30.860
Right now the center of mass,
the proton energy is 6.5 TV.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:33.710
The previous concept
and construction

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.530
had started in Texas
in the United States

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:40.550
in the so-called SSC tunnel,
which was much, much larger,

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:44.270
had 87 kilometer tunnel.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:46.230
The idea was to get
protons to center

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:51.050
at energies of 20 TV with
magnets of 6.8 tesla.

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:56.770
OK, this is one limitation,
the size of your tunnel.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:16:01.130
The second one is when you
bend a charged particle

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:03.140
with the magnetic
field, it radiates,

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:08.080
and it radiates proportional
with the energy proportion

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.320
energy over mass to
the fourth power, which

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:17.190
means that you, at some point,
are limited by the power you

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.200
have to invest in the
beam to even just keep it

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:21.000
at a specific energy.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:27.220
So this is e over m and
to the fourth power,

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:31.020
and this also gives you
a clue why we actually

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:34.330
accelerate protons in the
LHC and not electrons.

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:37.630
Electron mass is to low, and
this goes to the fourth power

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:38.410
here.

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:42.580
The [INAUDIBLE] of
electrons in the LHC tunnel

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:43.782
is just a limiting factor.

00:16:43.782 --> 00:16:46.240
At some point, you don't have
enough power anymore in order

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.310
to give that to the
electrons or the particles

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:49.980
in order to accelerate that.

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.560
All right, so as I
was saying initially,

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.650
so you need focusing,
you need accelerating,

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:03.880
and you need all kinds
of additional components

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:07.750
to accelerate in order to
make good colliding beams,

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:12.280
and so it's much easier to just
dump a beam into a fixed target

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:13.780
and study what's coming out.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:18.160
So colliding beams as a source
for input into particle physics

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.599
experiments came a
little bit later.

00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:26.260
First concepts came to fruition
in Frascati in the 1960s,

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:30.640
and then we had SPEAR, a
electron positron collider.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:33.370
It sent off mass energy of
four GeV, which then led

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:36.730
to the discovery of the J/Psi.

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:42.070
And then later, we had a five
GeV electron positron collider,

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:45.610
which was then
designed for eight GeV.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:51.890
This then continued,
as you know,

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:55.000
to machines like
[INAUDIBLE],, which

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:58.990
had a center of mass
energy of up to 110 GeV.

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:03.780
And the LHC, the center of mass
energy of design of 14 GeV.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:07.230
So the collider elements,
what do you need?

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:10.630
You need to inject
particles, and so you

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.550
have to have a
source of electrons

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:13.870
and a source of positrons.

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:17.230
Producing positrons is
just, technologically

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:20.540
in large numbers, much more
complicated than electrons.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:27.630
Here you can-- in order to
get sufficient electrons,

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:31.320
you need to actually
produce them

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.750
with some kind of an
accelerator structure as well.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:36.540
And so then they
need to be focused.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.370
They need to be
cooled down in order

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:42.730
to have them as a beam being
injected in the structure.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.000
So you've got-- you need
as additional components,

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.550
you need your RF generators.

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:51.960
You need magnets to bend
the beam in and out.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.930
You need to have magnets in
order to bend the beam around,

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:57.120
so we need bending magnets.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:00.897
You need focusing magnets
to keep the beam in orbit,

00:19:00.897 --> 00:19:02.730
and then you want to--
before you bring them

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:05.520
to the collision, we want to
further focus the beam such

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:09.540
that you have more actual
particle collisions available.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:11.070
And then you have
interaction points

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:13.440
where you will put your
particle for the experiment.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.260
What's being done
here in this facility

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:22.960
is you inject, you
accelerate, and then you

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:25.690
store the beam to make
sure that you can fully

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:31.210
exploit the structure, so those
rings are called storage rings.

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:36.010
You avoid having a one kind
of shot kind of mentality

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:39.550
as you have, for example,
in linear machine.

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:41.240
Sometimes you do this
in separate rings.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.660
Sometimes you do this
in the same ring.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:46.030
It depends on the
design of the machine.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:48.880
The challenges are if you
have particles travel around

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:53.400
for hours that they shouldn't
interact with the gas,

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:55.410
for example, in your
accelerator structure.

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:58.290
[INAUDIBLE] by a very,
very good vacuum in order

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:02.550
to not lose the beam
as you have it stored.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:05.040
The fields of your
accelerator structures

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.350
need to be very stable,
and that stable--

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:09.930
they have to be stable
for many, many hours.

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:12.570
That, for example,
there's some requirements

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:16.980
on your electric grid,
for example, such

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:21.600
that you have the stability
and voltage which don't end up

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.720
changing the actual field
strength of your magnet

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:27.930
as you go along.

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.690
Yeah, so again, I
already mentioned

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.600
this, the further development
of those machines.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:39.090
In the '80s at CERN,
stochastic cooling

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:44.160
was used for the first time
in an antiproton machine,

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:45.750
and that machine
led to the discovery

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:51.270
of the w and the z bosons
and first really deep study

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:55.770
of the weak interaction at the
scale of the weak interaction.

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:59.970
At the TEVATRON
close to Chicago,

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:01.590
protons and antiprotons
were brought

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:06.570
to collision at the Fermilab at
the Fermi National Accelerator

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.010
Laboratory.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:13.170
And the lab started in the
late 1980s in the same tunnel

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:16.650
as we find today the LHC.

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:22.620
At DESY in Hamburg, HERA was
used to collide electrons

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:26.070
with protons, and those results
led to our understanding

00:21:26.070 --> 00:21:27.940
of the structure of the proton.

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:31.080
So you see that the reason
why I show you this history

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:35.910
here is that the progress
we did in particle physics

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:39.630
at the energy frontier, each
of the energy frontiers,

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:43.380
was very much tied to the
progress in accelerator

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:44.370
structures.

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:47.790
And rightfully so,
Simon van der Meer

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:50.910
recieved the Nobel Prize in
physics as an accelerator

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:55.440
physicist for the discovery
of the w and z boson.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:57.750
He was working on
the machine and made

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:01.080
the machine possible
together with Carlo Rubbia.

00:22:04.896 --> 00:22:08.020
And interesting, if you look
at the history of the particle

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:12.400
accelerators, it's interesting
to see how we may move

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:13.880
the energy frontier forward.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:15.280
So what you see
in this plot here

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:19.470
is the available energy of the
machine, and what you see here

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:20.110
is line.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:22.000
And this project is called
the Livingston Project.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.417
See the logarithmic
scale, and you

00:22:23.417 --> 00:22:27.670
saw that for a very long time
it was this logarithmic increase

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:28.810
in available energy.

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:30.790
So this line is
now turning over.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:34.790
You're Already in 2020, and we
haven't made any progress here.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:39.010
So focus here on this.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:42.540
However, interesting
discussions on going somewhere--

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:47.130
going to high, going somewhere
here with the next machine

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.860
and being able to probe
further higher energies.

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:53.100
And the way this is proposed
for proton-proton collision

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:56.370
was just making a structure
similar to the LHC

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.110
but with a radius
about four times

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:02.190
larger, about 100 kilometers
compared to the 27

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:04.617
kilometers the LHC as today.

00:23:04.617 --> 00:23:07.200
And so you can compare this with
the hadron machines, but also

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:10.480
the electron machines, and
also on the electron machines,

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:15.540
this trend is not as pronounced
as for the proton machines.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:18.790
But basically, here, the
highest energy electron positron

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:21.690
collider [INAUDIBLE] has
just been decommissioned

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:24.420
at the end of the last
century, and we are thinking

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:26.910
about the next machine.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:31.780
Sometimes you see those
acronyms as a linear machine,

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:35.680
international linear collider
or future circular collider.

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:37.395
It's electron and
positron collisions,

00:23:37.395 --> 00:23:38.770
which would be
hosted in the very

00:23:38.770 --> 00:23:42.790
same tunnel as this machine,
which is called FCCPP or HH.

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:51.340
HH was 100 kilometer
circumference.

